10.38.263.113 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\"action\":\"replace_decision\",\"content\":\"# Solace Water Recovery Decision\\n\\n## Authority, scope, and restoration decision\\n\\nAURORA establishes that emergency restoration authority is distinct from public-health and communication closure authority [AURORA]. The authorized owner for execution is the Emergency Operations Director, who must explicitly accept each blocking control before proceeding. NEXUS confirms that readiness requires current candidate-bound evidence for every blocking control, independent review of residual risk, and explicit acceptance by the authorized owner [NEXUS]. No artifact, semantic register, mechanical check, or submission proposal can self-authorize readiness [NEXUS]. The scope covers all fourteen source objects' domains: hydraulic pressure and storage [BASTION], sampling and clearance [CIPHER], pump capacity [DELTA], power continuity [ECHO], treatment barriers [FALCON], contamination hypotheses [GARNET], access and control integrity [HELIX], telemetry [INDIGO], environmental permits [JASPER], logistics [KESTREL], public advisories [LUMEN], candidate lineage [MOSAIC], and independent review [NEXUS]. The restoration decision is bounded to the current candidate W7 (H12, P9, T6, W4) [MOSAIC] and cannot proceed until all three blocking findings—second-round samples, generator transfer evidence, and PHO acknowledgment—are resolved with current-candidate evidence [NEXUS]. Falsifier: if the authorized owner does not provide explicit acceptance, no readiness claim is valid regardless of check status [AURORA, NEXUS].\\n\\n## Pressure, storage, isolation, residence time, and candidate-bound hydraulics\\n\\nBASTION governs hydraulic pressure, storage volume, zone isolation, and residence time under candidate W7 (H12) [BASTION]. INDIGO provides the telemetry binding: pressure warning at 38 psi versus isolation trigger at 30 psi; these are distinct thresholds where warning does not equal action [INDIGO]. The p95 delay is 700 ms and p99 is 1,200 ms, which bounds control-loop timing [INDIGO]. Critical-signal coverage is 96 percent, meaning four percent of signals lack proven instrumentation confidence [INDIGO]. BASTION observations become stale if MOSAIC candidate changes hydraulic model H12 or valve map V8 [MOSAIC]. The residence time in storage must remain sufficient for treatment contact under FALCON plan T6; a change in DELTA demand ramp alters flow and thus residence time, staling both BASTION and FALCON evidence [DELTA, FALCON]. Owner: Hydraulic Operations Lead. Inputs: INDIGO telemetry at current config, BASTION zone map H12. Resources: isolation valves per V8, storage tanks per H12. Timing: continuous monitoring with p95 700 ms response. Dependencies: INDIGO coverage currency, MOSAIC candidate W7 stability. Effects: zone isolation upon 30 psi trigger; residence time adjustment for treatment contact. Observations: 96% coverage, current pressure readings per INDIGO. Contingency: manual valve override if telemetry gap exceeds uninstrumented zones. Rollback: restore V8/C11 compatibility to revert to W6 hydraulics [MOSAIC]. Retirement: supersede when H13 or later model is validated. Falsifier: if any zone pressure drops below 30 psi, isolation must activate; failure to isolate falsifies the hydraulic control claim [BASTION, INDIGO].\\n\\n## Sampling rounds, spacing, detection limits, action thresholds, and staleness\\n\\nCIPHER defines sampling rounds, spatial spacing, laboratory detection limits, action thresholds, and evidence staleness for clearance [CIPHER]. The critical distinction is between reporting limits (laboratory detection floor) and action thresholds (public-health trigger); a non-detect at the reporting limit does not equal an action-threshold clearance [CIPHER]. GARNET source-path separation requires sampling each materially different intake path: reservoir (VOC non-detect) versus river intake (3.1 µg/L measured) [GARNET, CIPHER]. The 34 percent ash-plume forecast is a probability estimate, not a measured concentration; it must not be conflated with the 3.1 µg/L river measurement [GARNET]. Second-round samples are incomplete, which is a blocking finding per NEXUS [NEXUS, CIPHER]. Staleness triggers: any change in source-water blend (GARNET), treatment plan T6 (FALCON), or demand ramp P9 (DELTA) invalidates prior sampling evidence [CIPHER, MOSAIC]. Owner: Laboratory Clearance Coordinator. Inputs: sample chain-of-custody per HELIX immutable logging [HELIX], detection limits per CIPHER protocol. Resources: laboratory capacity, certified operators from KESTREL mutual aid arriving 06:00 UTC ±90 min [KESTREL]. Timing: second-round completion before any clearance claim. Dependencies: GARNET investigation hold on chemical yard trench must be resolved before source-path claims are final [GARNET]. Effects: clearance status per zone; advisory state input to LUMEN [LUMEN]. Observations: current non-detect at reservoir, 3.1 µg/L at river intake [GARNET]. Contingency: extend sampling interval if blend changes; hold clearance pending second-round. Rollback: re-sample under prior candidate if W7 changes source paths [MOSAIC]. Retirement: supersede when next sampling round completes with current-candidate evidence. Falsifier: if any sample exceeds action threshold, or if VOC is detected at the reservoir (currently non-detect), source-path separation and clearance claims are falsified [CIPHER, GARNET].\\n\\n## Pump capacity, shared-main limits, staged demand, and current evidence\\n\\nDELTA bounds pump capacity, shared-main flow limits, staged demand ramp, and cavitation constraints under plan P9 [DELTA]. Nameplate capacity is not shared capacity; the effective limit is the minimum of individual pump rating and shared-main hydraulic constraint [DELTA]. Staged demand must respect KESTREL staffing: six certified operators available at 06:00 UTC ±90 min, which bounds the operational window for pump sequencing changes [KESTREL, DELTA]. Current evidence is bound to candidate W7 (P9); any change to demand ramp stales BASTION residence time and FALCON contact time simultaneously [DELTA, MOSAIC]. Owner: Pump Operations Lead. Inputs: pump performance curves per P9, shared-main hydraulic model H12 [BASTION], staffing availability per KESTREL [KESTREL]. Resources: pumps per P9 plan, operator shifts per KESTREL. Timing: staged ramp within staffing window; cavitation checks before each stage. Dependencies: ECHO power availability (rated vs usable) must support pump load regime [ECHO, DELTA]; KESTREL diesel covers 36 hours emergency load versus 22 hours full pump load—these regimes are distinct and must not be swapped [KESTREL]. Effects: flow restoration to zones per staged ramp; pressure feedback to INDIGO/BASTION. Observations: current P9 plan evidence under W7 [MOSAIC]. Contingency: reduce stage if power drops below usable 6.5 MW (rated 8.2 MW) [ECHO]; defer stages if staffing unavailable. Rollback: revert to prior demand schedule within P9 envelope; full rollback to W6 requires V8/C11 compatibility [MOSAIC]. Retirement: supersede when P10 or later plan is validated under current candidate. Falsifier: if cavitation occurs at any stage, or if shared-main flow exceeds hydraulic limit, pump capacity claim is falsified [DELTA].\\n\\n## Grid, generator, fuel, staffing, chemicals, and resource contingencies\\n\\nECHO establishes grid rated power at 8.2 MW with usable power at 6.5 MW; the generator provides 4.1 MW for 36 hours at current fuel stock [ECHO]. Rated versus usable power must remain distinct; the 1.7 MW gap is not available capacity [ECHO]. KESTREL diesel stock covers 36 hours at emergency load and 22 hours at full pump load—these two consumption regimes are separate and must not be conflated [KESTREL, ECHO]. Generator transfer evidence is stale for switchgear SW-9, which is a blocking finding [NEXUS, ECHO]. Chemical stocks: coagulant covers 4.5 days at current source blend; chlorine covers 3.2 days; inventory duration is not delivery lead time [KESTREL]. Staffing: six certified operators from mutual aid arrive at 06:00 UTC ±90 min [KESTREL]. Owner: Resource Continuity Lead. Inputs: fuel gauge readings, chemical inventory logs per KESTREL, staffing roster [KESTREL], switchgear SW-9 transfer test evidence [ECHO]. Resources: diesel tank, chemical storage, operator pool. Timing: 36-hour generator window from current fuel; chemical windows of 4.5 days (coagulant) and 3.2 days (chlorine). Dependencies: ECHO duration is controlled by KESTREL fuel; FALCON treatment staging depends on chemical stock [KESTREL, ECHO, FALCON]; DELTA pump sequencing depends on certified staffing [KESTREL, DELTA]. Effects: power continuity for treatment and pumping; chemical availability for disinfection. Observations: current fuel and chemical levels per KESTREL logs [KESTREL]. Contingency: if generator transfer fails under SW-9, fallback to manual switch; if fuel drops below 22-hour full-pump threshold, reduce pump load to emergency regime [ECHO, KESTREL]. Rollback: restore prior fuel/chemical ordering if consumption regime changes; revalidate SW-9 transfer before claiming continuity. Retirement: supersede when new fuel delivery or chemical replenishment is confirmed. Falsifier: if generator transfer fails under SW-9, or if chlorine residual drops below 0.8 mg/L entry due to stock depletion, power and treatment continuity claims are falsified [ECHO, KESTREL, FALCON].\\n\\n## Treatment barriers, turbidity, disinfectant, source blend, and contact time\\n\\nFALCON governs treatment barrier performance under plan T6: turbidity at 0.18 NTU with a gate of 0.30 NTU sustained for 4 hours; chlorine entry at 0.8–2.0 mg/L and distribution residual at or above 0.2 mg/L [FALCON]. Entry versus distribution thresholds are distinct; the 0.8 mg/L floor applies at treatment exit, while 0.2 mg/L applies in the distribution network [FALCON]. Contact time is a function of flow rate (DELTA P9) and storage volume (BASTION H12); any change to either stales FALCON verification [FALCON, DELTA, BASTION]. Source blend from GARNET (reservoir non-detect versus river 3.1 µg/L) determines coagulant dosing within KESTREL's 4.5-day stock window [GARNET, KESTREL, FALCON]. Owner: Treatment Process Lead. Inputs: turbidity sensor per INDIGO, chlorine residual per CIPHER sampling, flow per DELTA P9, storage volume per BASTION H12. Resources: treatment plant under T6, chemical dosing per KESTREL. Timing: continuous monitoring; 4-hour turbidity gate window. Dependencies: GARNET source-path investigation hold (chemical yard trench uninspected) must be resolved before blend claims are final [GARNET]; CIPHER second-round samples must confirm disinfectant efficacy [CIPHER]. Effects: treated water quality meets entry and distribution thresholds; contact time maintained for UV/chlorine inactivation. Observations: current turbidity 0.18 NTU, chlorine within range [FALCON]. Contingency: if turbidity exceeds 0.30 NTU for 4 hours, activate backup filtration or reduce flow to extend contact time; if chlorine drops below 0.8 mg/L entry, increase dose within KESTREL stock limit [FALCON, KESTREL]. Rollback: revert dosing and flow to prior T6 parameters; full rollback to W6 treatment requires revalidation under H12/P9 compatibility [MOSAIC]. Retirement: supersede when T7 or later plan is validated. Falsifier: if turbidity exceeds 0.30 NTU for 4 hours, or if chlorine residual falls below 0.8 mg/L at entry or 0.2 mg/L in distribution, treatment verification is falsified [FALCON].\\n\\n## Contamination hypotheses, observations, locations, investigation holds, and falsifiers\\n\\nGARNET defines contamination hypotheses tied to wildfire disturbance: ash-plume forecast at 34 percent intake impact (a probability estimate, not a measured concentration) and source-path separation between reservoir (VOC non-detect) and river intake (3.1 µg/L measured) [GARNET]. The investigation hold on the chemical yard trench remains uninspected; this hold must be resolved before source-path claims are final [GARNET]. Forecast versus observation is a critical distinction: the 34 percent figure is model-derived, while 3.1 µg/L is laboratory-measured; conflating them invalidates risk assessment [GARNET]. Micrograms per liter (µg/L) must not be swapped with milligrams per liter (mg/L); the river measurement at 3.1 µg/L is six orders of magnitude below a 3.1 mg/L reading [GARNET]. Owner: Environmental Investigation Lead. Inputs: watershed monitoring data, laboratory results per CIPHER, rainfall and plume dispersion models [GARNET]. Resources: field inspection teams (constrained by KESTREL staffing), laboratory confirmation. Timing: investigation hold active until trench inspection is complete; forecast updates with meteorological data. Dependencies: CIPHER sampling must confirm or refute VOC presence at reservoir [CIPHER, GARNET]; HELIX access controls constrain field team movement near chemical yard [HELIX]. Effects: source-path determination feeds into FALCON blend control and LUMEN advisory state [FALCON, LUMEN]. Observations: current non-detect at reservoir, 3.1 µg/L at river intake, trench uninspected [GARNET]. Contingency: if trench inspection reveals contamination, activate isolation per BASTION and extend CIPHER sampling; if rainfall event occurs, re-evaluate plume forecast. Rollback: revert to prior source-path model if new data contradicts current hypothesis. Retirement: supersede when investigation hold is resolved with evidence. Falsifier: if VOC is detected at the reservoir (currently non-detect), or if trench inspection reveals uncontrolled release, source-path separation and contamination hypotheses are falsified [GARNET, CIPHER].\\n\\n## Access, token and key state, immutable custody, retention, and automation restoration\\n\\nHELIX governs access control, token and key state, immutable evidence custody, retention schedules, and automation restoration under key set W4 [HELIX]. The SCADA token was revoked at 14:10 UTC; the controller key is current under W4; the break-glass credential expires in 2 hours with dual approval required [HELIX]. Immutable logging is mandatory for all control actions; retention is 90 days online and 7 years for incident records [HELIX]. Remote automation restoration requires named evidence of authorization, not merely a green check or provenance record [HELIX, NEXUS]. Owner: Security and Access Control Lead. Inputs: token/key inventory per W4, access logs under immutable custody, authorization chain per AURORA. Resources: key management system, break-glass procedure with dual approval. Timing: break-glass expires in 2 hours; token revocation is immediate. Dependencies: AURORA must authorize any remote automation restoration [AURORA, HELIX]; MOSAIC candidate W7 binds key set W4—any key-set mutation stales all access evidence [MOSAIC, HELIX]. Effects: controlled access to SCADA and treatment systems; audit trail for all actions. Observations: current token revoked, controller key active under W4, break-glass pending expiry [HELIX]. Contingency: if break-glass expires without use, initiate re-authorization through AURORA; if key-set mutation is required, coordinate with MOSAIC for candidate update and recheck [MOSAIC, HELIX]. Rollback: revert to prior key set only within W6 compatibility window (V8/C11) [MOSAIC]; reissue tokens under current W4. Retirement: supersede when next key rotation occurs under MOSAIC governance. Falsifier: if any unlogged access event is detected, or if break-glass is used without dual approval, access integrity claim is falsified [HELIX].\\n\\n## Telemetry coverage, warnings versus actions, delays, alternates, and uncertainty\\n\\nINDIGO provides telemetry coverage at 96 percent critical-signal confidence; the remaining 4 percent lacks proven instrumentation [INDIGO]. Pressure warning at 38 psi is distinct from isolation trigger at 30 psi; a warning does not 