13.59.360.770 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\"action\":\"replace_decision\",\"content\":\"# Solace Water Recovery Decision\\n\\n## Authority, scope, and restoration decision\\nAURORA separates emergency restoration authority from public-health and communication closure authority [AURORA]. The Emergency Operations Director executes restoration; the Public Health Officer (PHO) holds independent closure authority [AURORA]. NEXUS confirms readiness requires current candidate-bound evidence for every blocking control, independent review of residual risk, and explicit acceptance by the authorized owner [NEXUS]. No artifact, check, or submission self-authorizes readiness [NEXUS]. Scope covers all fourteen source domains under candidate W7 (H12, P9, T6, W4) [MOSAIC]. Three blocking findings remain: incomplete second-round samples [CIPHER], stale generator transfer evidence for switchgear SW-9 [ECHO], and missing PHO acknowledgment [AURORA, NEXUS]. Falsifier: without explicit owner acceptance, no readiness claim is valid regardless of mechanical check status [AURORA, NEXUS].\\n\\n## Pressure, storage, isolation, residence time, and candidate-bound hydraulics\\nBASTION governs hydraulic pressure, storage volume, zone isolation, and residence time under H12 [BASTION]. INDIGO binds telemetry: pressure warning at 38 psi versus isolation trigger at 30 psi; warning is not action [INDIGO]. p95 delay is 700 ms, p99 is 1,200 ms [INDIGO]. Coverage is 96 percent; the remaining 4 percent lacks proven instrumentation [INDIGO]. BASTION evidence stales if MOSAIC changes H12 or valve map V8 [MOSAIC]. Residence time must remain sufficient for FALCON contact under T6; DELTA demand ramp changes alter flow and stale both BASTION and FALCON [DELTA, FALCON]. Owner: Hydraulic Operations Lead. Inputs: INDIGO telemetry, BASTION zone map H12. Resources: isolation valves per V8, storage tanks per H12. Timing: continuous with p95 700 ms response. Dependencies: INDIGO currency, W7 stability. Effects: zone isolation at 30 psi; residence adjustment for treatment. Observations: 96% coverage, current pressures [INDIGO]. Contingency: manual valve override in uninstrumented zones. Rollback: restore V8/C11 compatibility for W6 hydraulics [MOSAIC]. Retirement: supersede when H13 validated. Falsifier: if any zone drops below 30 psi without isolation, hydraulic control claim is falsified [BASTION, INDIGO].\\n\\n## Sampling rounds, spacing, detection limits, action thresholds, and staleness\\nCIPHER defines sampling rounds, spacing, detection limits, action thresholds, and staleness [CIPHER]. Reporting limits (lab floor) differ from action thresholds (public-health trigger); non-detect at reporting limit is not clearance [CIPHER]. GARNET requires sampling each materially different path: reservoir (VOC non-detect) versus river intake (3.1 µg/L measured) [GARNET, CIPHER]. The 34 percent ash-plume forecast is probability, not concentration; do not conflate with 3.1 µg/L measurement [GARNET]. Second-round samples are incomplete—blocking per NEXUS [NEXUS, CIPHER]. Staleness: source blend change (GARNET), T6 change (FALCON), or P9 ramp change (DELTA) invalidates prior sampling [CIPHER, MOSAIC]. Owner: Laboratory Clearance Coordinator. Inputs: chain-of-custody per HELIX [HELIX], detection limits per CIPHER. Resources: lab capacity, certified operators from KESTREL mutual aid at 06:00 UTC ±90 min [KESTREL]. Timing: second-round completion before clearance. Dependencies: GARNET chemical yard trench hold must resolve before source-path claims final [GARNET]. Effects: zone clearance status feeds LUMEN advisory state [LUMEN]. Observations: reservoir non-detect, river 3.1 µg/L [GARNET]. Contingency: extend interval if blend changes; hold clearance pending second-round. Rollback: re-sample under prior candidate if W7 changes paths [MOSAIC]. Retirement: supersede when next round completes with current evidence. Falsifier: if any sample exceeds action threshold, or VOC detected at reservoir, source-path and clearance claims falsified [CIPHER, GARNET].\\n\\n## Pump capacity, shared-main limits, staged demand, and current evidence\\nDELTA bounds pump capacity, shared-main limits, staged demand, and cavitation under P9 [DELTA]. Nameplate capacity is not shared capacity; effective limit is minimum of individual rating and shared-main constraint [DELTA]. Staged demand respects KESTREL staffing: six certified operators at 06:00 UTC ±90 min [KESTREL, DELTA]. Evidence bound to W7 (P9); demand ramp change stales BASTION residence and FALCON contact simultaneously [DELTA, MOSAIC]. Owner: Pump Operations Lead. Inputs: P9 curves, H12 shared-main model [BASTION], KESTREL staffing [KESTREL]. Resources: pumps per P9, operator shifts [KESTREL]. Timing: staged ramp within staffing window; cavitation checks before each stage. Dependencies: ECHO usable power 6.5 MW (rated 8.2 MW) must support load regime [ECHO]; KESTREL diesel covers 36 h emergency versus 22 h full pump—distinct regimes, do not swap [KESTREL]. Effects: staged flow restoration; pressure feedback to INDIGO/BASTION. Observations: current P9 evidence under W7 [MOSAIC]. Contingency: reduce stage if power drops below usable 6.5 MW [ECHO]; defer stages if staffing unavailable. Rollback: revert within P9 envelope; full W6 rollback requires V8/C11 compatibility [MOSAIC]. Retirement: supersede when P10 validated. Falsifier: cavitation at any stage or shared-main flow exceeding hydraulic limit falsifies capacity claim [DELTA].\\n\\n## Grid, generator, fuel, staffing, chemicals, and resource contingencies\\nECHO establishes grid rated 8.2 MW, usable 6.5 MW; generator 4.1 MW for 36 hours at current fuel [ECHO]. Rated versus usable power must remain distinct; the 1.7 MW gap is unavailable [ECHO]. KESTREL diesel covers 36 h emergency load and 22 h full pump load—separate regimes, do not conflate [KESTREL, ECHO]. Generator transfer evidence stale for SW-9: blocking finding [NEXUS, ECHO]. Chemical stocks: coagulant 4.5 days, chlorine 3.2 days at current blend; inventory duration is not delivery lead time [KESTREL]. Staffing: six certified operators from mutual aid at 06:00 UTC ±90 min [KESTREL]. Owner: Resource Continuity Lead. Inputs: fuel gauges, chemical logs [KESTREL], SW-9 transfer test evidence [ECHO]. Resources: diesel tank, chemical storage, operator pool. Timing: 36-h generator window; chemical windows 4.5 d coagulant, 3.2 d chlorine. Dependencies: ECHO duration controlled by KESTREL fuel; FALCON staging depends on chemical stock [KESTREL, ECHO, FALCON]; DELTA sequencing depends on staffing [KESTREL, DELTA]. Effects: power continuity for treatment and pumping; chemical availability for disinfection. Observations: current fuel and chemical levels [KESTREL]. Contingency: manual switch if SW-9 transfer fails; reduce pump load to emergency regime if fuel below 22-h threshold [ECHO, KESTREL]. Rollback: restore prior fuel/chemical ordering; revalidate SW-9 before claiming continuity. Retirement: supersede when new delivery confirmed. Falsifier: generator transfer failure under SW-9, or chlorine below 0.8 mg/L entry due to stock depletion, falsifies continuity [ECHO, KESTREL, FALCON].\\n\\n## Treatment barriers, turbidity, disinfectant, source blend, and contact time\\nFALCON governs treatment under T6: turbidity 0.18 NTU with gate ≤0.30 NTU for 4 h; chlorine entry 0.8–2.0 mg/L, distribution residual ≥0.2 mg/L [FALCON]. Entry versus distribution thresholds are distinct: 0.8 mg/L floor at treatment exit, 0.2 mg/L in distribution [FALCON]. Contact time depends on flow (DELTA P9) and storage (BASTION H12); changes to either stale FALCON verification [FALCON, DELTA, BASTION]. Source blend from GARNET determines coagulant dosing within KESTREL 4.5-day window [GARNET, KESTREL, FALCON]. Owner: Treatment Process Lead. Inputs: turbidity sensor [INDIGO], chlorine residual [CIPHER], flow [DELTA], storage [BASTION]. Resources: treatment plant T6, chemical dosing [KESTREL]. Timing: continuous; 4-h turbidity gate. Dependencies: GARNET trench hold must resolve before blend claims final [GARNET]; CIPHER second-round must confirm efficacy [CIPHER]. Effects: treated water meets entry and distribution thresholds; contact time maintained. Observations: turbidity 0.18 NTU, chlorine in range [FALCON]. Contingency: if turbidity exceeds 0.30 NTU for 4 h, activate backup filtration or reduce flow; if chlorine below 0.8 mg/L entry, increase dose within stock limit [FALCON, KESTREL]. Rollback: revert dosing and flow to prior T6 parameters; W6 rollback requires revalidation under H12/P9 [MOSAIC]. Retirement: supersede when T7 validated. Falsifier: turbidity >0.30 NTU for 4 h, or chlorine <0.8 mg/L entry / <0.2 mg/L distribution, falsifies treatment verification [FALCON].\\n\\n## Contamination hypotheses, observations, locations, investigation holds, and falsifiers\\nGARNET defines contamination hypotheses: ash-plume forecast 34% intake impact (probability, not concentration) and source-path separation between reservoir (VOC non-detect) and river intake (3.1 µg/L measured) [GARNET]. Chemical yard trench remains uninspected—investigation hold active; must resolve before source-path claims final [GARNET]. Forecast versus observation is critical: 34% is model-derived, 3.1 µg/L is lab-measured; conflating invalidates risk assessment [GARNET]. Micrograms per liter (µg/L) must not swap with milligrams per liter (mg/L); 3.1 µg/L is six orders below 3.1 mg/L [GARNET]. Owner: Environmental Investigation Lead. Inputs: watershed monitoring, CIPHER lab results, plume models [GARNET]. Resources: field teams (constrained by KESTREL staffing), lab confirmation. Timing: hold active until trench inspection; forecast updates with meteorological data. Dependencies: CIPHER must confirm or refute VOC at reservoir [CIPHER, GARNET]; HELIX access constrains field movement near chemical yard [HELIX]. Effects: source-path determination feeds FALCON blend and LUMEN advisory [FALCON, LUMEN]. Observations: reservoir non-detect, river 3.1 µg/L, trench uninspected [GARNET]. Contingency: if trench reveals contamination, isolate per BASTION and extend CIPHER sampling; if rainfall occurs, re-evaluate plume forecast. Rollback: revert to prior source-path model if new data contradicts. Retirement: supersede when hold resolves with evidence. Falsifier: VOC detected at reservoir, or trench reveals uncontrolled release, falsifies source-path and contamination claims [GARNET, CIPHER].\\n\\n## Access, token and key state, immutable custody, retention, and automation restoration\\nHELIX governs access, token/key state, immutable custody, retention, and automation restoration under W4 [HELIX]. SCADA token revoked 14:10 UTC; controller key current under W4; break-glass expires in 2 h with dual approval [HELIX]. Immutable logging mandatory; retention 90 d online, 7 y incident [HELIX]. Remote automation restoration requires named authorization evidence, not merely a green check or provenance record [HELIX, NEXUS]. Owner: Security and Access Control Lead. Inputs: token/key inventory W4, access logs under immutable custody, AURORA authorization chain. Resources: key management system, break-glass procedure with dual approval. Timing: break-glass expires in 2 h; token revocation immediate. Dependencies: AURORA must authorize remote automation restoration [AURORA, HELIX]; W7 binds W4—any key-set mutation stales all access evidence [MOSAIC, HELIX]. Effects: controlled SCADA/treatment access; audit trail for all actions. Observations: token revoked, controller key active under W4, break-glass pending expiry [HELIX]. Contingency: if break-glass expires unused, re-authorize through AURORA; if key-set mutation needed, coordinate MOSAIC candidate update and recheck [MOSAIC, HELIX]. Rollback: revert to prior key set only within W6 compatibility (V8/C11) [MOSAIC]; reissue tokens under W4. Retirement: supersede at next key rotation under MOSAIC governance. Falsifier: unlogged access event, or break-glass used without dual approval, falsifies access integrity [HELIX].\\n\\n## Telemetry coverage, warnings versus actions, delays, alternates, and uncertainty\\nINDIGO provides 96% critical-signal coverage; 4% lacks proven instrumentation [INDIGO]. Pressure warning 38 psi distinct from isolation trigger 30 psi; warning is not action [INDIGO]. Delays: p95 700 ms, p99 1,200 ms bound control-loop response [INDIGO]. Coverage percentage is not confidence; uninstrumented zones remain unproven [INDIGO]. Owner: Telemetry and Monitoring Lead. Inputs: sensor network status per INDIGO config, signal quality metrics. Resources: telemetry infrastructure, backup sensors for critical gaps. Timing: continuous with p95/p99 bounds. Dependencies: BASTION isolation depends on 30 psi trigger [BASTION, INDIGO]; FALCON feedback depends on turbidity/chlorine sensors [FALCON, INDIGO]. Telemetry config change stales prior coverage evidence [INDIGO, MOSAIC]. Effects: real-time monitoring feeds hydraulic, treatment, and safety decisions. Observations: 96% coverage, current delay metrics [INDIGO]. Contingency: if coverage drops below 96%, activate alternate sensors or manual readings; if delay exceeds p99 1,200 ms, flag control-loop risk. Rollback: restore prior telemetry config if change introduces gaps. Retirement: supersede when next validation complete. Falsifier: critical signal lost without detection, or isolation trigger fails at 30 psi due to gap, falsifies monitoring adequacy [INDIGO, BASTION].\\n\\n## Environmental determination, notice clock, permits, discharge, and evidence custody\\nJASPER governs environmental permitting, discharge controls, 72-hour notice clock, and evidence retention under permit E-17 [JASPER]. Notice clock starts at materiality determination by AURORA, not initial detection; this distinction is critical for compliance timing [JASPER, AURORA]. Dechlorination residual above 0.019 mg/L at outfall stops discharge; hard permit condition [JASPER]. Evidence custody requires 7-year retention with provenance per JASPER [JASPER]. Owner: Environmental Compliance Lead. Inputs: permit E-17 conditions, outfall monitoring data, AURORA materiality determination record. Resources: outfall sensors, laboratory confirmation for residual chlorine. Timing: 72-h clock from AURORA determination; continuous outfall monitoring. Dependencies: AURORA must make materiality determination to start clock [AURORA, JASPER]; FALCON dechlorination process must maintain residual ≤0.019 mg/L [FALCON, JASPER]. Effects: permit compliance maintained; discharge authorized only within E-17 conditions. Observations: current outfall residual pending measurement; materiality determination not yet made [JASPER, AURORA]. Contingency: if residual exceeds 0.019 mg/L, stop discharge immediately and notify per E-17 emergency provisions; if materiality determination delayed, escalate to AURORA owner. Rollback: revert dechlorination settings if residual trend increases; revalidate outfall compliance after any FALCON plan change [MOSAIC]. Retirement: supersede when permit conditions change or discharge ceases permanently. Falsifier: residual >0.019 mg/L at outfall, or notice not provided within 72 h of determination, falsifies permit compliance [JASPER].\\n\\n## Public advisories, accessibility, alternate water, service states, and communications\\nLUMEN governs public advisories, accessibility, alternate water provision, service states, and communications under current advisory state [LUMEN]. Do-not-drink advisory reached 91% endpoints; call center capacity 3,200 contacts/hour for 6 hours versus forecast demand 4,100 contacts/hour [LUMEN]. Service states are distinct: do-not-use, do-not-drink, boil-water, restricted, cleared; low pressure is not contamination and must not trigger a false advisory state [LUMEN]. Owner: Public Communications Lead. Inputs: CIPHER clearance status per zone, BASTION pressure status, LUMEN endpoint reach data. Resources: call center capacity, alternate water distribution points, multilingual messaging. Timing: continuous advisory updates; 6-h call center window from current staffing. Dependencies: CIPHER second-round completion before any state change to cleared [CIPHER, LUMEN]; BASTION pressure restoration before ending do-not-use state [BASTION, LUMEN]. Effects: public informed of service status and alternate water locations; call center manages inquiries within capacity. Observations: 91% endpoint reach, current advisory state do-not-drink [LUMEN]. Contingency: if call demand exceeds 3,200/hour, activate overflow routing or extend staffing window per KESTREL [KESTREL, LUMEN]; if clearance achieved, transition to boil-water with updated messaging. Rollback: revert advisory state if new contamination evidence emerges [GARNET, CIPHER]. Retirement: supersede when all zones cleared and advisory retired. Falsifier: if advisory state does not match actual service condition, or if endpoint reach drops below 91% without documented"}
